		{
			"id": "113034472467982609",
			"parentId": "113034404211590501",
			"url": "https://mas.to/@williamoconnell/113034472432993146",
			"author": {
				"name": "William O'Connell",
				"url": "https://mas.to/@williamoconnell",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/293/897/760/366/326/original/5eb264bbdee235cb.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-27T14:32:51.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I've never been lucky enough to have something that was this popular so I can't really speak from experience, but I'm a little confused by why it's causing problems for so many sites. I would think any CDN should be able to solve this, even a really short TTL like 30 seconds would reduce the flood to just a few requests.</p><p>Usually when testing an application I'm happy if it can handle 100-200 requests per second, but for public content I would always have a CDN in front.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
