		{
			"id": "113035992850225764",
			"parentId": "113034463853080322",
			"url": "https://noc.social/@jmhobbs/113035979388918621",
			"author": {
				"name": "John Hobbs",
				"url": "https://noc.social/@jmhobbs",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/365/643/112/093/823/original/c06a0b7573edfdb2.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-08-27T20:56:05.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I would think with session reuse you wouldn't pay that TLS connection cost constantly. Also I imagine OpenSSL/BoringSSL has improved in the last seven years, and more cryptographic operations are done in CPU now then back then.</p><p>That said I don't think I'd run anything without a caching server in front anymore, unless it was very server interactive and would miss constantly. Varnish did amazing things at Flywheel.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "113046317427360432",
			"parentId": "113035992850225764",
